":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"/home/yarin.b/projects/excel_magic/artifacts/runs/20261002T174654Z_J6_qwen-code_breast_cancer/workspace/NOTES.md","content":"# Pipeline Optimization Notes - Breast Cancer Dataset\n\n## Best Performing Pipeline\nThe best performing pipeline achieved a 1-auroc score of 0.00402 (from the initial score of 0.00418).\n\n## Key Improvements Implemented\n1. Created engineered features based on domain knowledge:\n   - Ratios between mean and worst measurements for key features (radius, texture, perimeter, area)\n   - Spread features (difference between worst and mean values)\n   - Log-transformed features for skewed distributions\n   - Complexity metrics combining concavity, concave points, and compactness\n\n2. Feature Engineering Strategy\n   - Focused on creating meaningful ratios that might capture differences between benign and malignant tumors\n   - Used spread features to capture variability in measurements\n   - Applied log transformations to reduce skewness in highly skewed features\n   - Created compound features that combine multiple measurements\n\n## Lessons Learned\n- The initial pipeline with 30 features was already quite effective\n- Adding engineered features didn't significantly improve performance in this case\n- The TabPFN v2 model with the frozen configuration is already quite robust\n- Simple, well-chosen engineered features work better than complex combinations\n\n## Next Steps\nSince we've reached the limit of our evaluation budget, I'll stick with the best-performing pipeline (0.00402 score) which was achieved with the first engineered version."}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","usage":{"input_tokens":35016,"output_tokens":404,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"total_tokens":35420}}},{"type":"user","uuid":"0050db09-1268-410c-b960-9cc8b003db26","session_id":"f19a5335-0297-4bcc-a5b4-99e90a6e206a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_c3b4cab71d0343a3ad7f91c0","i